# UI Design Standards

## CSS Variables — Always Use Tokens

Never hardcode colors, shadows, or borders. Use the CSS custom properties defined in `App.css`:

```css
/* ✅ GOOD */
background: var(--bg-primary);
color: var(--text-secondary);
border: 1px solid var(--border-color);
box-shadow: var(--shadow-md);

/* ❌ BAD */
background: #ffffff;
color: #666;
border: 1px solid #ddd;
```

### Available token groups

| Group | Variables |
|-------|-----------|
| Backgrounds | `--bg-primary`, `--bg-secondary`, `--bg-tertiary` |
| Text | `--text-primary`, `--text-secondary`, `--text-muted` |
| Accent | `--accent-color`, `--accent-hover` |
| Borders | `--border-color`, `--border-color-light` |
| Shadows | `--shadow-sm`, `--shadow-md` |
| Glow (neon) | `--glow-accent`, `--glow-accent-strong` |
| Status | `--success-color`, `--error-color` |

## Neon Glow — `--glow-accent` / `--glow-accent-strong`

The "Neon" theme category (neon, volt, plasma, pink, ice, flare) is defined by an
outer accent **glow**, not just cyan-on-black colors. The glow is a *token*, so it
is opt-in per element and automatically **empty (`none`) on every non-neon theme** —
never gate glow with a JS theme check or a hardcoded `box-shadow`.

| Token | Use for |
|-------|---------|
| `--glow-accent` | Standard accent glow (`box-shadow`) — active/selected states, primary buttons, focus rings, live/status indicators |
| `--glow-accent-strong` | Layered hero glow (ring + halo) — the single primary CTA or featured/selected card on a screen |
| `--glow-text` | Text bloom (`text-shadow`) — brand wordmark, headings, accent-colored labels. **Never on body/paragraph text** (readability + paint cost) |

```css
/* ✅ GOOD — glows only under neon themes, no-op elsewhere */
.tab.active { box-shadow: var(--glow-accent); }
.primary-cta { box-shadow: var(--glow-accent); }

/* ❌ BAD — one-off hardcoded neon that ignores the theme system */
.card.active { box-shadow: 0 0 18px #22F0FF; }
```

**Rules:**
- Reserve glow for **signal-carrying** elements (primary CTA, active/selected state,
  focus, live indicators, brand mark). Do **not** glow every card, input, or row —
  full-screen glow hurts readability.
- `--glow-text` bloom goes only on high-contrast display text (wordmark, headings,
  accent labels) sitting on the near-black neon background. Never apply it to body
  copy — it must not lower any text/background contrast ratio (WCAG AA).
- Each neon theme derives its glow from its own `--accent-color`, so a single token
  reference lights up in the correct color for every variant.
- Values live only in `App.css` (`:root` = `none`; each neon `[data-theme=...]` block
  overrides). Never redefine the glow inline or per-component.

## Form Inputs

- Prefer `--bg-secondary` for field backgrounds (use `--bg-tertiary` when the field sits on a `--bg-secondary` card).
- Keep the **same** background on `:focus` and `:-webkit-autofill` — do not allow the browser to flash white.
- For discrete numeric values, use `NumberStepper` (`src/client/components/NumberStepper.tsx`) instead of `<input type="number">`.

## Dark Mode

Dark mode is handled automatically by `[data-theme="dark"]` in `App.css`. Do not add manual dark-mode overrides via inline styles or JavaScript theme checks:

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
const isDark = document.body.dataset.theme === 'dark';
style={{ color: isDark ? '#fff' : '#000' }}

// ✅ GOOD — use a CSS variable and let the theme do the work
style={{ color: 'var(--text-primary)' }}
```

## Co-located Stylesheets

The project uses a **two-tier CSS strategy**:

**Simple / new components** → CSS Modules (`.module.css`):

```
src/client/components/
  WorkItemCard.tsx
  WorkItemCard.module.css   ← import styles from './WorkItemCard.module.css'
```

Reference classes with `styles['class-name']` (bracket notation for kebab-case) or `styles.camelCase`:

```tsx
<div className={styles['work-item-card']}>
  <span className={styles.title}>...</span>
</div>
```

**Large / shared components** (ScrumCalendar, ReleaseView, CloudCost, DevStats, DetailsPanel, etc.) → plain `.css` with a side-effect import. These files are too complex for CSS Modules and use global class names intentionally:

```tsx
import './ScrumCalendar.css';
// classNames stay as plain strings: className="calendar-cell"
```

When creating a **new** component, always use CSS Modules. Do not convert existing large `.css` files to modules without explicit instruction.

## Inline Styles

Reserve inline styles for values that are truly dynamic (driven by runtime state). Use CSS classes for everything else:

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — dynamic value from state
style={{ opacity: isDragging ? 0.5 : 1, width: `${panelWidth}px` }}

// ❌ BAD — static values belong in CSS
style={{ padding: '8px 12px', borderRadius: '4px' }}
```

## Transitions

Use consistent timing:

- **Micro-interactions** (hover, focus, button states): `transition: all 0.2s ease`
- **Panel / modal animations** (open/close, slide): `transition: all 0.3s ease`

## Typography

Do not override the system font stack. The project uses:

```css
font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, sans-serif;
```

## Spacing & Sizing

Align spacing with existing components — avoid arbitrary magic numbers. Prefer values from the existing scale (4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32 px).

## Data grids (admin tables)

For tabular list views with filters and search, use the shared **Data Grid** pattern — do not invent a one-off layout.

- Components: `DataGridToolbar`, `DataGridFilterPills`, styles in `DataGrid.module.css`
- Layout: filter pills (default) or `DataGridFilterSelect` dropdowns when appropriate on the **left**, search on the **right** (same as Interviews)
- Table: dedicated **Actions** column with ghost buttons; primary identifier column is **not** clickable
- Procedure: load `.cursor/skills/data-grid-ui/SKILL.md` when adding or substantially changing a data grid
